import ConceptLayout from '../../layouts/ConceptLayout.astro'
---
<ConceptLayout
	slug="email-and-files"
	source={[
		'apps/web/src/api/services/email.ts',
		'apps/web/src/api/services/storage.ts',
		'apps/web/src/api/routes/files.ts',
		'apps/web/src/api/routes/uploads.ts',
		'apps/web/src/api/services/uploads/service.ts',
		'apps/web/src/api/services/uploads/targets.ts',
		'apps/web/src/db/schema/pending-uploads.ts',
		'apps/web/src/db/schema/files.ts',
		'packages/shared/src/files.ts',
	]}
>
	<h2>Email: one fetch call to Resend, no SDK</h2>
	<p>
		Sending mail is one function over plain <code>fetch</code> against Resend's HTTP API. There is no
		vendor SDK in the bundle, because the whole surface being used is a single POST with a JSON body
		— and an SDK would be a dependency, a bundle-size cost and a Node-compatibility risk in exchange
		for nothing.
	</p>
	<p>
		Templates share a shell and a call-to-action helper, and cover the magic link, the organisation
		invitation, the invitation-accepted notice and the access-request decision. The from address and
		the branding come from configuration, so changing them is not a code change.
	</p>

	<h2>With no API key, mail is logged rather than failed</h2>
	<p>
		With no mail provider configured, a message is not an error. It is written to the log — loudly,
		with any link printed on its own line — and reported as delivered-false. Nothing throws, nothing
		retries, and no code path branches on whether email "works".
	</p>
	<div class="why">
		<p>
			<strong>Why degrade instead of fail.</strong> This is what makes a fresh clone signable-in
			with zero external credentials. Sign-in is a magic link; a magic link is an email; an email
			with no provider would normally be a hard stop before the first screen. Treating the absent
			provider as a delivery channel of last resort — your terminal — removes the only credential
			that would otherwise be mandatory on day one.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>Which emails are queued, and which are sent inline</h2>
	<p>
		Invitation mail — created, bulk, resent — and the access-request decision notice are queued.
		Those routes answer as soon as the row exists, and a provider outage becomes a retry with
		backoff rather than a failed request.
	</p>
	<p>
		The magic link is sent inline, on purpose. Somebody is sitting in front of the login form
		waiting for it, and moving it to a queue trades a latency they can feel for a durability they
		would never notice. When the work has a human waiting on it, latency wins.
	</p>
	<div class="note">
		<p>
			The queued payload carries the link explicitly, so the console fallback still prints a usable
			accept URL when no provider is configured. The development path and the production path go
			through the same handler.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>File storage: a small interface over R2</h2>
	<p>
		File storage sits behind a small service interface — put, get, head, delete, list — whose one
		implementation wraps the native R2 binding. Keys are built by a helper and always take the shape
		<code>tenants/&lbrace;tenantId&rbrace;/&lbrace;scope&rbrace;/&lbrace;uuid&rbrace;-&lbrace;filename&rbrace;</code>,
		so a single prefix scopes one tenant, or one scope within it, for listing or bulk deletion. The
		UUID means every key is unique whatever the client happened to call the file.
	</p>
	<p>
		Downloads stream through the Worker, so every download passes the same organisation and
		visibility checks as every other route. Uploads do not have to: the next section is how a file
		gets in.
	</p>

	<h2>Uploads: ask, put, confirm</h2>
	<p>
		An upload takes three requests. The client first asks <code>POST /api/uploads</code> for a
		place to put the file, naming the kind of upload, the file's name, type and size. Every check
		that kind makes runs here, before a byte moves: permission, type, size, an empty file, and
		whether storage (and, for a document, conversion and embeddings) is configured at all. The
		answer is a <code>PUT</code> URL that lasts 10 minutes, recorded in a
		<code>pending_uploads</code> row.
	</p>
	<p>
		The client then <code>PUT</code>s the bytes with the type and length it declared, and asks
		<code>POST /api/uploads/:id/confirm</code>. Confirm checks the stored object's size and type
		against the declaration — a mismatch is refused and the object deleted — and only then writes
		the <code>files</code> row, or the document. An upload belongs to its organisation and the
		person who started it: anyone else's upload id is a 404, and so is a second confirm. A nightly
		task deletes the objects of uploads nobody confirmed.
	</p>
	<p>
		Where the <code>PUT</code> goes depends on one setting, and the client cannot tell which. With an
		R2 API token's key pair set, the Worker presigns a URL on R2's S3 API (the binding itself cannot
		presign), signing the type, the length and write-once, and the file goes straight to the bucket.
		Without the key pair the URL is the Worker's own, which holds the same rules itself; deployed,
		it takes at most 10 MB a file. Provisioning writes the bucket's CORS rule, and local
		development needs no R2 credentials.
	</p>
	<div class="why">
		<p>
			<strong>Why direct uploads are opt-in.</strong> The <code>FILES</code> binding cannot presign, so
			a direct URL is signed against R2's S3 API and needs an R2 API token. Without one, every upload
			goes through the Worker's own URL, which holds the same rules, up to 10 MB a file deployed.
		</p>
	</div>
	<div class="note">
		<p>
			A direct upload is a single <code>PUT</code>, so 5 GiB is the ceiling, with no multipart
			upload and no resume. A presigned URL is not spent by the confirm: for its 10 minutes it can
			write the key again once the confirmed object is gone. The nightly task deletes such an
			object for the kit's own targets, and a plugin owns that cleanup for its own.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>The files table is the only thing the browser can name</h2>
	<p>
		A <code>files</code> table records id, tenant, uploader, scope, key, filename, content type,
		size and creation time. Rows are immutable: there is no <code>updated_at</code>, because a file
		is not edited, it is replaced by another file. The browser only ever references a file by that
		row's id — never by a storage key.
	</p>
	<p>
		Writes go object first and row second; if the insert fails, the object is deleted. Downloads
		are tenant-scoped lookups — another tenant's
		file is a 404, not a 403 — and carry an ETag from R2 so a repeat request is a 304.
	</p>

	<h2>Upload targets, and the download default</h2>
	<p>
		Each kind of upload is a target with its own rules: the kit's are <code>avatars</code>,
		<code>uploads</code> and <code>documents</code>, and a plugin adds its own. An avatar must be
		one of a short list of image types, every target is capped by size, and an empty file is
		rejected outright. Apart from the Worker's own upload URL, no core route takes a file in its
		request body.
	</p>
	<div class="why">
		<p>
			<strong>Why almost everything downloads as an attachment.</strong> Only the avatar image
			types are served inline. Everything else — SVG very much included — is sent with a
			<code>Content-Disposition: attachment</code> header, so a stored HTML page or a scripted SVG
			cannot execute on the application's own origin against a live session. Rendering user-supplied
			markup inline is a stored-XSS primitive; the attachment header removes it, and the cost is
			that one class of file downloads instead of previewing.
		</p>
	</div>
	<div class="note">
		<p>
			A missing storage binding is a loud 503 rather than a silent no-op. Realtime can degrade
			quietly because nothing is lost; storage cannot, because an upload that appears to succeed
			and stores nothing is data loss.
		</p>
	</div>
</ConceptLayout>
